# `slice` is bounds-checked at RUN time, the way `substring` is: the constant
# fold that refuses an out-of-range literal range on a constant string has no
# slice twin, so a bad range reaches the emitted guard and panics there.
# `to > length` is the half this pins; `from > to` panics through the same
# condition. A panic ends the run, so the check lives in its own program.
main: function is {
    ns: (ListVal i64)
    ns.append from: 10
    ns.append from: 20
    print "before"
    bad: ns.slice from: 1 to: 9
    print "unreachable \{bad.length}"
}
